  chef: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 556ecd75ee852ce4a93c60357c4eb4f6, type: 2}
  letterBody: 'Ciao!


    I am Giulia, from a small trattoria in Rome. A customer
    told me about your shop. "Six countries in one kitchen," he said, shaking his
    head. Then he said the food was good. So, I write.


    Aglio e olio is what
    Roman cooks eat at midnight, after service, when everyone is too tired to talk.
    Pasta, garlic, chili, good oil. Nothing else.


    Cook the garlic slowly until
    it is golden, never brown. Save a little pasta water. It makes everything come
    together.


    The dishes with the fewest things have nowhere to hide.'
